{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["With the help of the Former of light, I shall explain the meaning of the Torah, to unify His awesome name, who made me from רֵאשִׁית (beginning), and counseled me and called me רֵאשִׁית (first), to engage in the pleasantness of רֵאשִׁית. I begin to explain the Book of בְּרֵאשִׁית (Genesis)." "The heavens opened and I saw visions of Elohim, the Creator of the ends of the earth, and I meditated upon what I was permitted to meditate upon at the opening of words of holiness, and I awakened from its east — the Torah's east. <b>The verse's saying בְּרֵאשִׁית</b> — that the form of the word is construct (סְמוּכָה), as one says ״רֵאשִׁית דָּבָר״ (the beginning of a matter), and here the noun it depends on is missing — and who is like our Elohim, knowing a thing? For from Him comes forth a thing." "It says at the beginning of the order of creation <b>the heavens</b>, and our Sages of blessed memory explained the word as being compounded of two things: fire (אֵשׁ) and water (מַיִם). From this it would seem, heaven forbid, to give a handle to the transgressors to say that this is the beginning of creation, but the water and the fire the Creator did not create, heaven forbid — since the verse itself has made us hear that the beginning of creation is the heavens, and if the waters were created He should have preceded with their creation. And our Sages of blessed memory (Ḥagigah 12a) were guarded against two objections and explained that the verse's measure is on this pattern: 'At the beginning of the creation of heaven and earth, the earth was unformed' etc. — not that the beginning of creation was heaven and earth. But their words of blessed memory did not suffice to remove the error, for according to them, why did the verse not afterward inform us of the creation of water and fire and dust and spirit? And although the verse says (Genesis 1:9) ״יִקָּווּ הַמַּיִם״ etc. ״וְתֵרָאֶה הַיַּבָּשָׁה״, it is not heard as though it was created, and it is far more correct according to the implication that it already existed, except that He decreed upon it to be revealed when the waters would be gathered." "There is a fine point to examine in the verse's saying <b>et the heavens and et the earth</b>: what do these two instances of et come to include?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the verse's intention is along this path. And beforehand I shall arouse the dawn presented to us concerning the greatness of the Creator — how great are His deeds! For the Master, blessed be He, is able to speak a speech not grasped by created beings, as they said of blessed memory (Mekhilta, Yitro) in explanation of the verse (Exodus 20:1) ״וַיְדַבֵּר אֱלֹהִים אֵת כָּל הַדְּבָרִים״ etc., and this is their language: 'matters that the mouth cannot speak' — and this is that He spoke all ten commandments in a single utterance, and no utterance preceded its fellow." "<b>And with this</b> we come to the explanation, which is that the Master, may His name be exalted — everything He created in the world, namely the sources and all that exists in reality, the heavens and the earth — He created all of it altogether in a single utterance. And this is what the verse says: <b>בראשית ברא אלהים את השמים ואת הארץ</b>. It carefully said two instances of et to include everything found in the world. And although the created beings are many — for besides the ten things that are recognizable and visible to all, as the Sages enumerated them (Hagigah 12a), and this is their language: ״Ten things were created on the first day: heavens and earth, light, darkness, tohu and vavohu, etc., water, spirit״ — end quote — besides these there are yet other creations hidden and absorbed within the powers of the elements; some are discernible through the process of smelting, some through the process of compounding, some through the changing of times, some through the potentiality of the hyle, and they are beyond counting. Therefore the Creator encompassed them all in two great and mighty particulars — in the heavens and in the earth — and these are the two inclusio-markers that He multiplied by saying <b>et the heavens and et the earth</b>, and in a single utterance He, may His name be exalted, created everything. And you will find that the Sages, of blessed memory, said (Rosh HaShanah 32a) that ״בראשית״ is an utterance, for by it they completed the count of ten utterances by which the world was created; the first of them all, through which everything was created — except that it was not yet arranged, and all the creations existed in existence but lacked order. And the Holy One, blessed be He, arranged them, each thing on its designated day. On the first day He prepared and separated the light from the darkness, and assigned the darkness its place and the light its place; and on the second day, etc." "<b>And the faithful witness</b> to this our explanation is what the verse says (Genesis 2:3): ״כי בו שבת מכל מלאכתו אשר ברא אלהים לעשות״ — which otherwise has no meaning. But with what we have explained, it yields a kind of sense: for on it He rested from all His work, which He had created on the day of creation לעשות — meaning, to arrange in six days, as it says (Exodus 31:17): ״כי ששת ימים עשה ה׳״ etc. — meaning, He arranged. For the creation on the first day was brought into being through a single utterance, except that everything lacked arrangement. To what may the matter be likened? To one who wished to build a palace, and first prepares stones and wood and earth and building materials in perfection, and afterward builds the house. So the Creator, blessed be He, created all the necessities of the world in a single utterance and prepared them, and everything lay heaped in confusion; and afterward He began to arrange each thing in its proper manner and accomplished His work, may His name be exalted." "<b>And you will find</b> that when He wished to create the heavens He said (Genesis 1:6): ״יהי רקיע בתוך המים״ — meaning, He fixed its place; but it had already been created by the first utterance. And so the earth (Genesis 1:9): ״ותראה היבשה״." "<b>And as for the fire,</b> which HaShem did not mention for its arrangement at all — know that HaShem did not disclose its source as He disclosed the heavens and the earth and the waters, because it is a powerful source, and were it in existence like water and earth, there would be from it destruction for the world, and the world could not endure. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, left it in the place where it was created by the first utterance, absorbed within the flint-stones, until a person needed it and struck the flint-stones and extracted from them enough fire for his needs, as they said, of blessed memory (Bereshit Rabbah 11), that therefore we recite on Motza'ei Shabbat ״בורא מאורי האש״ (Pesachim 54a). In this too HaShem revealed the manner of the intermixture — that the sources and elements were intermingled at the time of their creation, before HaShem stationed each one at its post. For had this thing not been in existence, the human mind could not have conceived how sources could be mixed together — all the more so earth and fire." "<b>And it is</b> true that the Creator could have included the arrangement as well in the first utterance, but for many reasons He did so, and one of them is that which the Sages, of blessed memory, revealed in their saying (Avot 5:1): ״והלא במאמר אחד יכול להבראות, אלא ליפרע וכו׳, וליתן שכר טוב לצדיקים״ etc." "And let it not be difficult in your eyes that they of blessed memory said (Avot 5:1): ״בַּעֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת נִבְרָא הָעוֹלָם״ — using the language of *beriah* (creation), for in the end, any time that the *tikkun* (arrangement) was lacking, it is as though there is no creation there. The analogy — if the earth had not been revealed, there would be no place for the created beings; and likewise the rest of the creations that were intermingled, such as light and darkness. If so, whatever was not arranged is as though it was not created; therefore creation is attributed to them as well." "<b>And let it not</b> be difficult in your eyes why the Torah wrote the essence of creation in this manner, and did not write explicitly: ״HaShem created the waters and the dust and the fire and the wind״ — for in this, no hollow person would be misled." "<b>Know</b>, that the Torah makes known and proclaims everything hidden, and HaShem desires to make known the power of His deeds to His people. He made known to us the order of creation and its manner — that He is the mighty El, girded with might, who created with one utterance every existent thing and all that comes into being. HaShem desires to make known to His beloved the power of His deeds, and He will not withhold this good for the sake of the fools who stray." "<b>Furthermore</b>, there is another reason that obligates speaking in the order stated, namely that if the verse had spoken according to the order the fools desire — that is, if it had said that He created earth and created waters, etc. — it would have been understood that He created each one separately, on its own. But the truth is otherwise. Do you not know that HaShem does not desire a speaker of lies, and no falsehood stands before His eyes — so how would He begin His words, heaven forbid, with things that are not so? Therefore He spoke the truth of the manner of creation, for ״the ways of HaShem are straight and the righteous walk in them״ (Hosea 14:10), to give thanks and to exalt the Creator blessed be He, and to say to Elohim: ״How awesome are Your deeds.״" "<b>Examine carefully</b> why it says <b>בְּרֵאשִׁית</b> and did not say *ba-rishonah* (in the beginning [feminine]). ״The power of His deeds He declared to His people״ (Psalms 111:6) — for according to what we have set forth, that He created everything with one utterance, by this path we will understand that one who speaks many things in a single utterance, even in that utterance, has no need to precede one letter before its companion. And from this point on, when HaShem said the word שָׁמַיִם, the *shin* did not precede the *mem*, nor the first *mem* precede the last *mem*, and likewise the letters of each and every word — the beginning of the word did not precede its end; and also in each and every letter, its beginning did not precede its end." "<b>And after</b> Elohim made known to us all this — these are the things that HaShem made known to us in the word <b>בראשית</b> — meaning: everything is reishit, for there was no later-created element in the creation; rather everything is reishit. And understand this." "<b>And the reason</b> the Master who chose to create in this manner — besides the reason we said, that He wished to make known a portion of His mighty acts, may He be blessed — you will further find that when He, may He be blessed, speaks through mighty acts, which is the aspect that relates to the name Elohim from the Name, this being so the things are spoken in this order. And you will find that they of blessed memory alluded to this (Makkot 24a) with the pleasantness of their words, and this is their language: ״אנכי ולא יהיה לך מפי הגבורה שמענום״ — end of quote. They intended to say, with the sweetness of their language, that the things spoken from this aspect — which is Elohim as relating to gevurah — are spoken as these things, many things at once; and not specifically אנכי and לא יהיה לך were said in one utterance, but rather all ten commandments were so, as stated in their words elsewhere (Mekhilta Yitro); only that what reached our perception was two of them. And see what we wrote in parashat Yitro (Exodus 20:1). And behold, the creation of the work of Creation was created with the name Elohim, as it is written ״ברא אלהים״ — for many reasons; therefore He spoke, in His way, may He be blessed, great and awesome things, as we have written." "And by way of derash, the word <b>בראשית</b> will be explained along the lines of what Scripture says (Psalms 33:6): ״בדבר ה׳ שמים נעשו וברוח פיו כל צבאם״. And you should know that the name of our Elohim, may He be exalted, did not create the angels until the second day, so that the mistaken ones would not err by thinking they assisted Him in the work of Creation. And according to their words, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 1), we must raise the question: how is it possible to say that the angels were created later? For according to what Scripture says, ״וברוח פיו כל צבאם״ — since the host of the heavens was created from the breath that issued from the mouth of the Most High, as it were — if so, necessarily, according to what is perceived, the angels would have been created before heaven and earth, for the breath precedes and goes out before the speech issues. And since the heavens and the earth were created from the speech, the breath — from which the angels came forth — had already preceded. Therefore Scripture says <b>בראשית ברא</b> etc. — meaning that HaShem brought forward what was later, which is the speech, ahead of the breath, the opposite of what is known to us in the nature of speech, and created the heavens first, and reversed the order, and made the speech reishit to the breath — for the reason stated in their words of blessed memory (ibid.), which is so that they should not say that the angels were helpers and stretched out the heavens with Him, etc. And Scripture informs us that the Master, blessed be He, cannot be assigned a measure from the created beings, for the created beings are unable to reverse the order implanted in them at the time of creation, whereas the Creator, blessed be He, is able to speak in whatever manner He desires. And therefore you will find that Scripture was precise in saying ״בדבר ה׳ שמים נעשו״ — placing the creation of the heavens, which were created from the speech, first — and afterward said ״וברוח פיו כל צבאם״." "<b>And now</b> the measure of the explanation of the word <b>בראשית</b> is along this path: for since within speech there are two things — the breath and the speech itself — and from each of them HaShem created a new creation, and I do not know which of the two preceded — whether the breath or the speech — therefore it says that reishit was the making of heaven and earth; and from the conclusion of the matter you know that the speech was reishit to the breath that issued from the mouth of the Most High." "Further, we must understand the difference between the two verses: in this verse He said only the name <b>אלהים</b>, while another verse says (Genesis 2:4) ״ביום עשות <b>ה׳ אלהים</b> ארץ ושמים״ — which mentions two names. And furthermore, here He placed heaven before earth, while there He placed earth before heaven." "<b>And it will be seen</b> that these two difficulties each resolve the other, along the lines of what the Sages, their memory for blessing, explained (Sotah 31a) regarding two verses written concerning the reward of the righteous: one verse says (Exodus 20:6): ״וְעֹשֶׂה חֶסֶד לַאֲלָפִים״, and one verse says (Deuteronomy 7:9): ״לְאֹהֲבָיו וּלְשֹׁמְרֵי מִצְוֹתָיו לְאֶלֶף דּוֹר״. And they said, their memory for blessing, that what the verse stated ״לַאֲלָפִים״ — is for those who love Him alone, and it does not extend as well to those who keep His commandments, who are those who act from fear, for them another verse mentioned that they are a thousand generations. Also the verse that said ״לְאֹהֲבָיו וּלְשֹׁמְרֵי מִצְוֹתָיו לְאֶלֶף דּוֹר״ — its statement ״לְאֶלֶף דּוֹר״ does not apply except to ״שֹׁמְרֵי מִצְוֹתָיו״ which follows it, and not to the word ״אוֹהֲבָיו״ that precedes it, for those — behold, their reward is with them in another verse, which is ״לַאֲלָפִים״." "<b>Also</b> we shall explain these two verses along this path: <b>בְּרֵאשִׁית בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים אֵת הַשָּׁמַיִם</b> — this name of Elohim means this attribute of strict judgment without joining the name of mercy; this applies specifically to that which pertains to the heavens, and does not extend to His saying ״וְאֵת הָאָרֶץ״, for the earth He created with the joining of the name of mercy, and it leans upon another verse, which is His saying (Genesis 2:4): ״בְּיוֹם עֲשׂוֹת ה׳ אֱלֹהִים אֶרֶץ״ — for the world of mankind cannot endure in pure judgment without the joining of mercy, since they are of material substance as is known. And His saying ״וְשָׁמָיִם״ is not drawn along with the earth in this aspect of joining, and the manner of its making is written in parashat Bereshit. And with this the two fine points are satisfactorily resolved, and the matter of knowing which came first — behold, we have written above that all of creation was created together by His power, may He be blessed." "And it should not be difficult for you — what justice does YHWH administer to the divine beings, inhabitants of the heavens, that it would be fitting to apply the name Elohim to them? Will you not find that they said, their memory for blessing (Chagigah 15a), concerning the affair of Metatron, that they judged him before Elisha and struck him with sixty lashes of fire, and also Elijah etc. (Bava Metzia 85b), and how many judgments there are for the ministering angels on high. And even though they have no evil impulse, with all this, error may be found in them, for sometimes they do not intend toward the truth and they err. Go out and learn from the affair of the angel Metatron and Elijah, for they erred; and so it may happen that YHWH will punish them even for unintentional error — something for which one who judges with this standard does not judge the inhabitants of earth. And with this what we explained is verified, and it is correct." "<b>And although</b> we find for our Sages, their memory for blessing (Bereshit Rabbah ch. 1), that they perceived the two fine points in the verses, and they resolved that the reason why in one verse He preceded the heavens and in the other He preceded the earth is that He intended to say that both emerged as one, and the reason why in one place He said Elohim and in another He joined the name of mercy — they said (Bereshit Rabbah ch. 12) that at first it arose in His thought to create with the name Elohim, and He saw that the world could not endure, so He joined etc.:" "<b>Know</b> that permission is granted to us to explain the meaning of the verses along the pathways of reasoning and settled understanding, even though the earlier ones preceded us and resolved in a different manner, for there are seventy faces to the Torah, and we are not warned not to deviate from the words of the earlier ones except in interpretations where the halakhic ruling would change according to them. And therefore you will find that the Amoraim have no authority to disagree with the Tannaim in matters of divine law, but in the resolution of verses and their meanings we find for them in several places that they explained in a different manner." "<b>Again</b>, I have looked with the discernment of inquiry into the verses, for they will bear our words as well as the words of our predecessors, upon them be peace. And first let us understand how it is justified in the Master, blessed be He, beginning and end in His thought — that at first He thought with the attribute of judgment, and then turned to partner [it with mercy]. For the Master, blessed be He — nothing is hidden from Him; in a brief moment and in less than the blink of an eye He knows all and directs to the truth at the first glance, as it were, blessed be He and blessed be His name." "<b>Now indeed</b>, know that the intention of partnering mercy bears two implications: the first, that HaShem has mercy at times upon whomever He wishes to have mercy without bringing him to judgment, for this is the attribute of mercy — to pardon and to forgive. And the name Elohim likewise — for one whom He does not wish to pardon, He brings to judgment. Accordingly, there is for you a person who does not come to judgment. The second path is that HaShem never grants exemption to any person in the world, and all the world will stand for judgment; none escapes. And you must know the statement of the Sages, their memory for blessing (Bava Kamma 50a), and this is their language: ״Anyone who says the Holy One, blessed be He, is indulgent — may his innards be indulgent; rather, He sets the earth in judgment, and no person escapes the judgment.״ Accordingly, we have chosen for ourselves the second path." "And from now on, the manner of creation according to their words, their memory for blessing, is as follows: at first He created with the attribute of Elohim, which is the attribute of judgment, and thus the ultimate purpose was that all would stand in judgment. Except that He saw that the world could not endure under that judgment, He partnered with it the attribute of mercy. That is: not that He turned back from His first thought, heaven forbid, but that He would carry out the judgment in a merciful way, as you would say — not to exact from a person his punishment all at once, and also not to exact it from him immediately without delay; and a person never receives anything other than what reaches him through judgment, and this is their statement, their memory for blessing (Yerushalmi Shekalim ch. 5): ״He lengthens His wrath, and [nevertheless] it is gathered to Him [for judgment].״" "<b>You therefore find</b> that He did not turn back from His thought, for we find no precedent in the thought of HaShem that He did not intend to conduct Himself with judgment in this manner — that He would exact payment through mercy. Accordingly, it is necessary for the verse to speak in the order stated; for if it had said ״בְּרֵאשִׁית בָּרָא ה׳ אֱלֹהִים״ the impression would be given that there is judgment and there is also, on the side of mercy, [something beyond] judgment, for HaShem did not decree judgment alone. But from His saying ״בָּרָא אֱלֹקִים״ alone, He has decreed that there is nothing without judgment; and although the verse afterwards says ״ה׳ אֱלֹהִים״ it is to be understood in its true sense as we have explained it — that the judgment will be in any case, except that the exacting will be in a merciful manner. And the verse relies on this, having compelled us to interpret in this manner, to establish as a firmly held matter in our hands that the knowledge of HaShem does not change, as it were." "<b>And after</b> having explained the intention of the Sages of blessed memory, even according to their words there is room for our interpretation, for this measure of partnership that HaShem prepared for the inhabitants of the world — to lengthen His anger and to collect little by little in full measure — HaShem does not conduct Himself thus except with us, and not with the supernal ones, the dwellers of heaven; rather, immediately and at once He pays the one who incurs liability his due all at once. Moreover, regret and confession and repentance do not avail them, nor does the claim of inadvertence. Go forth and learn from the incident recounted in the Talmud (Ḥagigah 15a) concerning the four who entered the Pardes, that the angel who sits in the retinue of the King, may His name be exalted, blessed be He, was punished because he did not stand before Elisha, so that the latter would not stray in his thought — and this for an angel [was only] inadvertence, and he was not warned about it. And likewise He does not judge the Judge of His people Israel in the manner known from the laws of His Torah, may it be exalted:" "There is further a mystical allusion in the precedence of the heavens to the earth, to show that the measure of the Holy One, blessed be He, is not like the measure of flesh and blood: the measure of flesh and blood is to build the house first and then the upper story upon it, but the Holy One, blessed be He, preceded the upper story and then the house, to show that the heavens are suspended and stand by His utterance, may He be blessed." "And by way of mystical allusion, our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Genesis Rabbah 1) the word בְּרֵאשִׁית as intending to say: for the sake of the Torah, which is called רֵאשִׁית (\"beginning\"), and the meaning of the בֵּי״ת of בְּרֵאשִׁית is the בֵּי״ת of causation. From this it follows: whoever has merited the Torah has merited the entire world, and whoever has not merited the Torah has no right to enjoy the world even to the width of a footstep, unless he uses it to facilitate his labor in it. And they also said (Leviticus Rabbah 36) for the sake of Israel, who are called רֵאשִׁית, as it is written: ״רֵאשִׁית תְּבוּאָתֹה״ (Jeremiah 2:3). And this saying of theirs does not contradict the first, for the very reason Israel are called רֵאשִׁית is on account of their receiving the Torah, and in this they were distinguished for a name and for praise above the nations. And they also said (Genesis Rabbah 12) for the sake of Abraham and for the sake of Jacob (Leviticus Rabbah 36), and all goes according to the first reason — ״עֵקֶב אֲשֶׁר שָׁמַע אַבְרָהָם״ etc. They also said (Shabbat 86a) for the sake of fear [of Heaven], which is called רֵאשִׁית, as it is written (Psalms 111:10): ״רֵאשִׁית חָכְמָה יִרְאַת HaShem״ — for this too is the foundation of the Torah, for if there is no wisdom there is no fear, and if there is no fear of HaShem there is no Torah, for it is a precious treasury for the Torah." "Further, <b>בְּרֵאשִׁית</b> may be interpreted by way of that which it says (Proverbs 8:30): ״וָאֶהְיֶה אֶצְלוֹ אָמוֹן״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted (Genesis Rabbah 1): ״Do not read אָמוֹן but אוֹמֵן (artisan/craftsman).״ And this is itself what the verse alluded to at the beginning of HaShem's speaking: when He made known the creation of the world, He made known with what He created it, and said — with the Torah, for it is the אוֹמֵן (the artisan). (This does not contradict the first interpretation, for the Torah prepared the world to walk in uprightness.) And according to this, the greatness of those who master it is exalted, for they merit everything. And therefore the fitting title for them is that they be called בּוֹנִים (builders), which is what they said of blessed memory (Shabbat 114a): ״Sages are called בּוֹנִים,״ for that which is in their mouth and in their heart is the בּוֹנֶה (that which builds), and this is what it says (Jeremiah 33:25): ״אִם לֹא בְרִיתִי״ etc. ״חֻקּוֹת שָׁמַיִם וָאָרֶץ לֹא שָׂמְתִּי״." "Further, the בֵּי״ת of בְּרֵאשִׁית may be interpreted by way of what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Introduction to Tikunei Zohar 5): there are two modes of the fear of HaShem. The first is fear of punishment, and the second is fear of exaltedness, and this is an inner fear, superior to the first. And this is what is alluded to in the letter בֵּי״ת of רֵאשִׁית — that for two (ב׳) fears HaShem prepared the world's existence. And He alluded to the manner of both: the first He alluded to in the word <b>אֱלֹהִים</b>, for it is the fear of judgment when He arises for judgment; and the second He alluded to in His saying <b>et the heavens and et the earth</b>, which follows the manner of what it says (Psalms 8:4): ״כִּי אֶרְאֶה שָׁמֶיךָ״ etc. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Zohar vol. 1, 1a) that fear of exaltedness enters the heart of an understanding person when he looks with contemplation upon the work of heaven and earth — how great are the deeds of HaShem — and the soul becomes inflamed and fears His greatness, may His name be blessed." "<b>Or</b> it may allude to two modes of fear: one in His saying <b>the heavens</b> — here He alluded to fear of exaltedness, by way of what they said ״כִּי אֶרְאֶה שָׁמֶיךָ״ etc.; and the second fear in <b>and et the earth</b>, which is fear of punishment, and He attributed it to the earth to indicate that it is not on account of exaltedness but on account of the awe He inspires among those born into the world, by way of what it says (Job 20:27): ״וְאֶרֶץ מִתְקוֹמָמָה לוֹ״." "It further alludes in <b>בְּרֵאשִׁית</b> along the lines of what is said (Isaiah 33:6): ״יִרְאַת ה׳ הִיא אוֹצָרוֹ״ — the intention is that whoever has fear of HaShem, it is a treasure for the hope of the hearts, which is the clinging of His light, may He be blessed, in a person's soul, as it says (Deuteronomy 4:4): ״וְאַתֶּם הַדְּבֵקִים״ and so forth; and whoever has fear of Heaven is a dwelling-place for the Shekhinah, as it says (Psalms 78:60): ״אֹהֶל שִׁכֵּן בָּאָדָם״. And this is what is alluded to in the word בְּרֵאשִׁית along this path: bet — meaning a house, the dwelling-place of Elohim — it is the fear alluded to in the word רֵאשִׁית; בָּרָא HaShem — and He prepared His work." "<b>בְּרֵאשִׁית</b> may further be interpreted along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Leviticus Rabbah 35:5): the book and the sword came down bound together, and so forth. And this is what is said <b>בְּרֵאשִׁית בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים</b> — meaning: with the רֵאשִׁית, which is the Torah, Elohim created — meaning the דִּין (judgment), which is the allusion to the sword." "It may further be interpreted along the lines of what the verse says (I Kings 18:39): ״ה׳ הוּא הָאֱלֹהִים״, and the intention is that even though the name Elohim relates to judgment, it is nevertheless mercy, and all the names of the Holy One, blessed be He, aim together toward good — to do good and to show kindness. Rather, on account of the deeds of the latter generations, judgment went forth to chastise whoever is fit to be chastised; but aside from this, even the name Elohim, its measure is to do good. And this is what is said <b>בְּרֵאשִׁית בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים</b> — for the name Elohim did good and created a world to sustain and to show compassion and to do good to the dwellers of heaven and earth, and there is no greater compassion than this. And this is what the verse says (Genesis 27:28): ״וְיִתֶּן לְךָ הָאֱלֹהִים״ — see how even the name Elohim will do good to the righteous by way of a free gift; except that the doers of wickedness cause it to turn about so that Elohim brings them chastisement. And in the time to come, the hoped-for expectation is that thus HaShem will be one and His name one." "The word <b>בְּרֵאשִׁית</b> may further be interpreted along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Zohar, Introduction 2a), that at the time of the world's creation the letters entered before the Creator. The letter tav entered and said before Him: Master of the Universe, it is Your will to create the world through me, and so forth — and HaShem answered all of them that He did not desire it, for the reason stated in their words concerning each and every one. Until the letter bet entered, and He received it and created the world through it, as it is written ״בְּרֵאשִׁית״. The aleph was silent. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to her: Aleph, why are you silent, and so forth. He said to her: You shall be the head of all the letters, and so forth — end of quote. Now, from the words of HaShem it would appear that aleph has superiority over bet, and from this it follows that if the letters had ascended before Him in order, the aleph would have merited to have the world created through it. And since one might ask: And why did the aleph not ascend first and leave her until the end? — know that they said, of blessed memory (Introduction to Tikkunei Zohar), that the letters when they are in forward order — along this path aleph, bet, gimel, and so forth — allude to the attribute of mercy; and when they are in reverse order — along this path tav, shin, resh, and so forth — they indicate the attribute of judgment. And this is your sign: the month of Tishrei, whose letters in reverse are tav, shin, resh, is the season of great judgment — except that HaShem, in His compassion, joined one letter from the name of mercy, namely yod, to temper the judgment (Introduction to Tikkunei Zohar 16). And after He has made this known to us, let us interpret the verse along this path: bet — רֵאשִׁית: know the reason for the precedence of bet over aleph, namely that the creation was in the attribute of Elohim (judgment); and since this is so, the letters ascended in reverse order as is the rule, and through this bet preceded aleph and took its place." "It further intends in saying <b>בְּרֵאשִׁית</b>, along the lines of what they said, of blessed memory (Zohar, Introduction), that a thousand (eleph) hidden worlds did HaShem create, and they call them, of blessed memory (Zohar Hadash, Genesis 2a), עָלְמִין דְּכִסּוּפִין (worlds of yearning), and they are alluded to in the verse (Song of Songs 8:12): ״הָאֶלֶף לְךָ שְׁלֹמֹה״. From this it follows that the verse began with bet, because the aleph had already taken its place — through it He created eleph (a thousand) worlds after the name of aleph — and He began with bet to create this world, since there is also in the reckoning of creation that they are two: heaven and earth. And for this reason as well it did not say ״בָּרִאשׁוֹנָה״, which would make you understand that this was the very beginning — but it is not so, for He had already created the worlds of desire; rather, it is specifically regarding this particular detail of heaven and earth that He says its רֵאשִׁית." "It further alludes, in the word <b>bereshit</b>, along the lines of what they of blessed memory said (Midrash Shmuel on Avot, ch. 6), that HaShem created four worlds, and they assigned them names: Atzilut, Beriah, Yetzirah, Asiyah. And they alluded to them in the verse (Isaiah 43:7): ״כֹּל הַנִּקְרָא בִשְׁמִי וְלִכְבוֹדִי בְּרָאתִיו יְצַרְתִּיו אַף עֲשִׂיתִיו״. And this is what He alluded to here by saying <b>bereshit</b> — this is the world of glory, which is Atzilut, and it is called reishit of everything; ״ברא״ — this is the world of Beriah; ״אֱלֹהִים״ — an allusion to the world of Yetzirah, for there are judgments there; ״אֵת הַשָּׁמַיִם וְאֵת הָאָרֶץ״ — this is the world of Asiyah, for it is literally heavens and earth, as stated in their pleasant words." "It further alludes along the lines of what they of blessed memory said (Pesachim 54a), that Gan Eden and Gehinnom were created before the world was created, for they are the reward and punishment for Torah and mitzvot. And this is what He alluded to by saying <b>two reishit</b> — for two things did HaShem create, unique, for the sake of yirah, which are reward and punishment: Gehinnom and Gan Eden (Chagigah 15a). One who merits and fears HaShem — takes his portion and his fellow's portion in Gan Eden; one who does not merit — takes his portion and his fellow's portion in Gehinnom. And perhaps it alludes that both were created together, and to both of them reishit applies, for if one had preceded the other, reishit would apply to only one; from this you learn that they were created together in a single utterance." "It further alludes that, since there are two worlds — this world and the world to come — and man, through his engagement in Torah and good deeds, attains the good and life, here Scripture informs us that the reward of Torah and mitzvot is not paid to the righteous in this world until He prepares for them the treasure of good, which is the essential portion of the soul, and afterward, if there is surplus, they benefit him also in this world. For HaShem chooses first to prepare for man the recompense of the supernal world, which is eternal, and this is what He alluded to by saying <b>bereshit bara Elohim et the heavens</b> — which are the world of souls — and afterward <b>and et the earth</b>, which is the lower world, if He deems it fitting in His goodness. From this you learn that the righteous are not benefited in this world until after they merit their inheritance to shine in the light of life." "It further alludes, based on what our Rabbis of blessed memory disagreed about (Rosh Hashanah 10b) — one said: the world was created in Tishrei, and one said: the world was created in Nisan. And it appears to me that these and those are words of the living Elohim, for you must know that they of blessed memory said (Bereshit Rabbah, ch. 1) that in the beginning HaShem thought to create the world, and afterward HaShem brought His thought into action. Accordingly, we can say that in Nisan He thought and in Tishrei it went into action, or the reverse, and to both of them the term beriah applies — both what arose in His thought, may He be blessed, is called beriah, and also when it went into action. And through this the words of our Rabbis are fulfilled together. And this is what He alluded to in the bet of the word <b>bereshit</b>, for there were two reishit — one for thought and one for action — except that we do not know whether He thought in Nisan and acted in Tishrei, or the reverse." "It further intends, along the lines of what they of blessed memory said (Berakhot 6a): whoever has fear of Heaven, his words are heard. And the reason is that one who fears the word of HaShem speaks from the very substance of his soul, and since that is so, the soul of the listener recognizes and receives the reproof of musar. And if the words come from the body, the soul has no capacity to hear, as they of blessed memory alluded in what Scripture says (Deuteronomy 6:6): ״וְהָיוּ הַדְּבָרִים וגו׳ עַל לְבָבֶךָ״ — then ״וְשִׁנַּנְתָּם לְבָנֶיךָ״ — these are the students. And this is what it says: <b>bereshit</b> — which is yirah, as they of blessed memory said — <b>bara HaShem et the heavens</b> — meaning, let him be one who fears Heaven, then his voice will be heard and he will teach man knowledge, and this is what it says: <b>and et the earth</b>:" "It further intends that HaShem commanded many forms of fear in the world: fear of one's father and mother, as it is written (Leviticus 19:3): ״אִישׁ אִמּוֹ וְאָבִיו תִּירָאוּ״; also fear of the king and of the sages; also to fear fellow creatures so that they not suspect him. And one might say that the commandment of fear should be equal in all places where it applies, and from this it would follow that sometimes — heaven forbid — he would slight the commandments of HaShem for the honor of the king or for the honor of his father and the like. Therefore the verse alludes here, at the beginning of the words of HaShem, that before all else one should precede to fear et the heavens and afterward et the earth — which are the particulars one is obligated to be mindful of. And this is what the verse says (Numbers 32:22): ״וִהְיִיתֶם נְקִיִּים מֵה׳״ first, and afterward ״וּמִיִּשְׂרָאֵל״. And from now on there is no room to fulfill any commandment of fear of others unless it does not violate any commandment of HaShem." "It further alludes that through the mediation of the Torah HaShem created two levels in man: either he is equal in level to the heavens, which is along the lines of what He said (Psalms 82:6): ״אֲנִי אָמַרְתִּי אֱלֹהִים אַתֶּם״; or if he does not uphold it, his level descends downward to the earth. And this is the meaning of <b>בְּרֵאשִׁית</b> — meaning, through the mediation of רֵאשִׁית, HaShem created these two preparations: et, etc." "The passage further alludes entirely to the creation of man, for he is the primary purpose of creation, in this manner: <b>בְּרֵאשִׁית</b> — when HaShem created man, He created him from the source of spirituality alluded to in the word ״הַשָּׁמַיִם״ and from the source of corporeality alluded to in the word ״הָאָרֶץ״. And you will find that our Rabbis of blessed memory alluded to this (Sanhedrin 91a) in what the verse says (Psalms 50:4): ״יִקְרָא אֶל הַשָּׁמַיִם מֵעָל״ — which is the spirit; ״וְאֶל הָאָרֶץ״ — which is the body built from the earth. And His blessed will in this is that through man's deeds he gains the power to transform the matter, which is the body, until it is reckoned as form." "And behold, you will find (Shabbat 152b) that the righteous do not return to dust, because they purified their matter until it became spiritual, as the Rav, Rabbi Yitzchak Arama of blessed memory, wrote, and this is his language: ״And the intensification of the perfect ones is to transform matter into form״ — end of his words. And therefore they of blessed memory said (Berakhot 18a) that the righteous in their death are called ״living,״ for the corporeal part has become spiritual, which is attributed to life; and the wicked in their lifetime are called ״dead,״ because even the spirituality within them has been corporealized and become earth, which is attributed to the inanimate, which is dead." "And these two traits He alluded to in His saying <b>et the heavens and et the earth</b>, in this manner: if they merited, then they bring near the corporeal part within them, which is alluded to by ״the earth,״ joining it so that it becomes one unity (homonoyy'a) with the heavens; and if they did not merit, then they transform the spiritual part within them to the aspect of earth, so that the form becomes matter — and this is the meaning of <b>and et the earth</b>." "And its saying <b>והארץ היתה תהו ובהו</b> — here is an allusion to what happens through sins, that the side of materiality, which is the earth, overcomes and departs from spirituality, as Targum renders it צדיא ורקניא — <b>וחשך</b>, which is the evil impulse, called in words of the holy ones (Zohar vol. I 63a) דמחשיך אנפי דבריאתא, <b>על פני תהום</b> — and He called the body תהום (the deep) because it is like the deep, constantly swallowing, and from the side of the impulse, which is the darkness, <b>ורוח אלהים</b> that is placed within it <b>מרחפת על פני המים</b> — for the spirit found no rest to settle its feet, for the impulse drove it out and it was raised from its resting place, and the darkness, which is the evil impulse, dwelled there, and the soul remained מרחפת על פני המים — these are the waters of presumption, which are the forces of impurity." "<b>And when</b> the Holy One, blessed be He, saw that it was so, <b>ויאמר אלהים יהי אור</b> — He sends to man help against his impulse, and this comes when he reaches fourteen years of age: there shines upon him from the abode of His holiness a spirit of holy divinity, a holy and pure soul. Or it may allude to the Torah, for it is the precious light, and it is called אור, as it is written (Proverbs 6:23) ״ותורה אור״. And the intent is one, for the aspect of holy souls is the aspect of the light of Torah, and one who possesses soul is one who possesses Torah. And this is what is said: <b>ויאמר אלהים יהי אור</b> — meaning the soul, whose light is great, hewn from beneath the Throne of His Glory, blessed be He, a divine portion from above — HaShem sends it to illuminate upon man, so that he not sink into the mire of the evil impulse. Or also for what we said, that it is the Torah that illuminates a person's soul, and then <b>ויהי אור</b>, <b>וירא אלהים את האור</b> which He placed in man <b>כי טוב</b> — for through its agency man walks in the good and upright path." "<b>And do not</b> think that when the soul is within man, or through engagement in Torah, he is thereby tranquil from the assaults of the impulse that leads him astray, which has turned and gone away — for if so, there is here neither reward nor punishment. Therefore it says that the darkness is still in the world; only that through this a person is aided to wage war against it and to separate it from himself, so that it not cause him to fall through its counsels, and to recognize the nature of doing evil, that it is indeed evil. And this is the meaning of <b>ויבדל אלהים בין האור</b>, which is the soul, <b>ובין החשך</b>, which is the evil impulse. And this is what the verse says (Ecclesiastes 10:2): ״לב חכם לימינו״ — which is the place of light, the place of the soul; ״ולב כסיל לשמאלו״ — which is the place of the evil impulse. And He called the light day and the darkness He called night, and therefore David prayed (Psalms 91) that He deliver him from terror in the nights." "<b>And its saying ״ויהי וגו׳ יום אחד״</b> — for it is impossible that the luminescence of the soul be recognized except from the darkness of the evil impulse; for if not so, one would not differ from the ministering angels, and there would be here neither reward nor punishment. And therefore it says ״יום אחד״." "<b>It further intends</b> to say <b>יום אחד</b> in the manner of their saying, of blessed memory (Avodah Zarah 35a), on the verse (Song of Songs 1:3) ״על כן עלמות אהבוך״ — ״על מות״, meaning: even an angel of evil answers Amen involuntarily. And therefore it says <b>ויהי ערב ויהי בקר יום אחד</b>, meaning: the attribute of evening and the attribute of morning are all in one agreement for the benefit of the righteous." "<b>Or</b> it may allude that by means of the assistance of the soul there will be ערב — the time when one's sun sets to depart to the supernal world — like בקר, the time of one's entry into the world, so that the soul returns shining as on the day it arrived. This is on the path of what they said, of blessed memory (Shabbat 152a): ״וְהָרוּחַ תָּשׁוּב אֶל הָאֱלֹהִים אֲשֶׁר נְתָנָהּ״ (Ecclesiastes 12:7), and this is their language: give it to Him just as He gave it to you. And in this, the day of one's arrival equals the day of one's departure as one." "Further, the verse intends to declare that at the beginning of creation HaShem prepared and made His handiwork equal — heavens and earth alike. For even though the heavens are the supernal ones, none­the­less the earth too was in the category of purity. Go out and learn its virtue and merit, for from it was man created — dust from the earth — and his form was like an angel of HaShem (Genesis Rabbah 8:9). And you will find that HaShem chose to dwell in the earth among the creatures, and prepared a house for His rest, as it were. And it is a simple matter, for the dwelling-place of Elohim is perfect and holy shall be said of it. And you will find that before man sinned, HaShem was present among the lower beings as among the supernal ones — and this is what the verse says: <b>בְּרֵאשִׁית בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים אֵת הַשָּׁמַיִם וְאֵת הָאָרֶץ</b> — in one category of cleanness and purity. And it is the word of the prophet (Isaiah 66:1): ״כֹּה אָמַר ה׳ הַשָּׁמַיִם כִּסְאִי וְהָאָרֶץ הֲדוֹם רַגְלָי״, and they said, of blessed memory (Chagigah 14a), that He is a seraph — you have now learned that it [the earth] is worthy of being the dwelling-place of Elohim. Except that through the deeds of the lower beings <b>וְהָאָרֶץ הָיְתָה תֹהוּ וָבֹהוּ</b> — it became materialized and its light was darkened on account of the deeds of the wicked. <b>וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים יְהִי אוֹר</b> — which is the Torah — then the earth returned to its former state to shine its light, and HaShem dwelt in the earth with His people and His inheritance, and this is what it says: <b>וְיְהִי אוֹר</b>." "Further, the verse will be expounded along this path: for it is a known matter that we find the Holy One, blessed be He, chose to dwell in the thick darkness (I Kings 8:12), and desired to dwell among us. And furthermore, we find (Ta'anit 5a) that, as it were, He swore not to enter the heavenly Jerusalem until He enters the earthly Jerusalem — this declares that the one below is more beloved to Him than the one above. Also, when we reflect on the reason for the creation of the entire world, we will know that the essential element is the lower beings — they are His people and His inheritance." "<b>And according to</b> this, the verse intends to state the order of creation as לא זו אף זו — not only this but also this — in a path of endearment: <b>בְּרֵאשִׁית בָּרָא אֱלֹהִים אֵת הַשָּׁמַיִם</b>, and further He created a creation more beloved to Him and more superior, and it is <b>אֵת הָאָרֶץ</b> — for the heavens, on the contrary, depend upon it. And all this is through the mediation of the children of Israel, the holy portion of His people (Deuteronomy 32:9), for upon them depends the maintenance of the worlds, including the supernal ones, as is known to those versed in the chambers of true wisdom." "<b>And according to</b> this path, His saying <b>וְהָאָרֶץ הָיְתָה תֹהוּ</b> will be expounded: here He hinted, by way of allusion, at the four exiles of Israel, which are the cause of the hindrance of His dwelling, may He be blessed, in the earth. The first exile is the exile of Egypt, in which there remained ת״ו [= 406] and thirty, according to the numerical value of תֹהוּ: ת״ו [= 406] plus eleven for וָבֹהוּ, whose reckoning is י״ט [= 19] — הרי [= total] ת״ל [= 430], as it is written (Exodus 12:40). And the meaning of <b>וָבֹהוּ</b> also alludes to the exile that followed it, which is the exile of Babylon; the exile of Babylon is called בֹהוּ because the Targum of בֹהוּ is רֵיקַנְיָא [\"empty\"], and it is written in Jeremiah (Jeremiah 51:34) concerning the exile of Babylon: ״אֲכָלַנִי הֲמָמַנִי נְבוּכַדְנֶאצַּר מֶלֶךְ בָּבֶל הִצִּיגַנִי כְּלִי רִיק״. And His saying <b>וְחֹשֶׁךְ עַל פְּנֵי תְהוֹם</b> alludes to the bitter exile in which we are sunk, in a תהום that has no end — this one upon us is one thousand and seven hundred and seventy-one [1771] years. And the length of the exile is not enough, but חֹשֶׁךְ alludes to two things: the first, to the subjugation of the nations and the heavy yoke of taxes, until their countenance is darker than black — this one oppresses and that one rules over them; happy is he who did not see [such things], especially in our West. And the second is concerning the evil inclination, which has darkened the world, and through our sins the transgressors among His people Israel have multiplied, and the license of impurity of the mouth and vain oaths and evil tongue and robbery has been given rein, and these prevent them from doing repentance so as to restore their captivity. And let no person say, seeing such decline of Israel all the length of time, that — Heaven forbid — hope from the children is lost. Not so! Rather, <b>וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים יְהִי אוֹר</b> — this is the light of the future, wondrous redemption." "And its saying <b>וַיְהִי אוֹר</b> — according to what they say in Tikkunei haZohar (Tikkun 21): ״וַיְהִי רָז״ — for אוֹר in gematria is רָז. The intention is that HaShem, may His name be exalted, decreed that this light of King Mashiach shall not be revealed in the world, and it shall remain a hidden secret with Him, as they said (Tikkunei Zohar, Tikkun 21): לְלִבִּי גִלִּיתִי, מִלִּבָּא לְפוּמָּא לָא גַּלֵּי — ״To my heart I have revealed; from my heart to my mouth I have not revealed.״ And its saying <b>וַיַּרְא אֱלֹהִים אֶת הָאוֹר כִּי טוֹב</b> turns back to the light from its own perspective — for it is good. Happy is the eye that saw! — and it also turns back to what it hinted, that He would make it in hidden secrecy — כִּי טוֹב, for so it must be, as they said, of blessed memory (Yoma 38b), for several reasons. And its saying <b>רוּחַ אֱלֹהִים</b> etc. — they said in the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 2): this is the spirit of Mashiach. <b>מְרַחֶפֶת עַל פְּנֵי הַמָּיִם</b> — ״waters״ means nothing but Torah, according to what they say in the Zohar (in the addenda to Part 1, 13), that Israel shall be redeemed only through the merit of Torah, which is likened to waters." "<b>And behold,</b> it is well known that because of our many sins, many sparks of holiness have sunk into the kelipot, and also many of the erev rav have become absorbed within the holiness, as they said in the holy book Tikkunei haZohar (Tikkun 66) — the bad is mixed with the good and the good with the bad. Therefore it is necessary, as it were, to separate the good from the bad and the light from the darkness that had become intermingled. Now it is known that the kelipah's vitality is specifically its drawing of nourishment from the holiness, and without this it has no vitality. Therefore when HaShem separated the light — which is the holiness — and the bad remained separated with no place of vitality left from which to draw nourishment, it would consequently be nullified. And this is what is said (Zechariah 13:2–3): ״וְאֶת רוּחַ הַטֻּמְאָה אַעֲבִיר מִן הָאָרֶץ״ — for it is like cutting a tree from its place of nourishment and the root from which it draws, so that it dries up and is of no further use, and nothing remains but the good light. And this is what is said: <b>וַיִּקְרָא אֱלֹהִים לָאוֹר יוֹם</b> — meaning, ״וַיִּקְרָא״ in the language of preciousness and greatness, for the excellence of holiness is recognized only in the felling of the kelipah, which is called ״darkness.״ And Scripture informed us that on that day known to Him in the heavens, HaShem will call the light with preciousness and greatness, and this shall be on the day that shall be known to HaShem, when He shall be in it One (Zechariah 14:9)." "And its saying <b>וְלַחֹשֶׁךְ קָרָא לָיְלָה</b> — this is in the language of an incidental occurrence, something impure. Therefore Scripture did not attach itself to the word ״וַיִּקְרָא״ that it said at first, for there is no longer preciousness or glory for the darkness; rather its occurrence shall be specifically at night. And this is the secret of what is said (Deuteronomy 23:11) ״מִקְּרֵה לָיְלָה.״ But when the night — which is the time of exile — passes, as Scripture says (Isaiah 21:11) ״מַשָּׂא דּוּמָה״ etc. ״שׁוֹמֵר מַה מִּלַּיְלָה״ — for exile is likened to night. And then, when the dawn rises, there is no more darkness and no night, but rather ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וַיְהִי בֹקֶר יוֹם אֶחָד״ — for the aspect of evil has departed and is no more, and the night shall be as bright as day, and the light of the moon shall be as the light of the sun. And this is what is said: <b>יוֹם אֶחָד</b> — and this is what Scripture hinted in saying (Zechariah 14:9) ״בַּיּוֹם הַהוּא יִהְיֶה ה׳ אֶחָד וּשְׁמוֹ אֶחָד.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The proem plays on the repeated word רֵאשִׁית through allusion — 'made me from reishit' echoing Proverbs 8:22, 'called me reishit' echoing Jeremiah 2:3, 'pleasantness of reishit' i.e. Torah per Proverbs 8:19 rabbinic reading — each transliterated to keep the wordplay visible.", 1 "The phrase 'awakened from its east (ממזרחה) of the Torah' plays on מזרח as east/dawn; rendered hyper-literally to preserve the dual image.", 4 "The lemma אָכֵן ('indeed') is taken here as the opening word of Or HaChaim's own comment, introducing the resolution, not as a lemma from the verse.", 35 "The citation from Psalms reads אֹהֶל שִׁכֵּן in the source text, though the standard verse reads שִׁילֹה; I have translated the text as the Or HaChaim quotes it.", 49 "The marginal notation [הוֹמוֹנְיָא] corrects the printed [חומוניא]; rendered here as 'unity (homonoyy'a),' reflecting the Aristotelian-Averroist term for ontological oneness that Arama and the Or HaChaim employ.", 59 "The Aramaic word רֵיקַנְיָא means 'empty,' linking בֹהוּ to Jeremiah's כְּלִי רִיק ('empty vessel'). The gematria of תו (406) plus 30 equals 430; the text unpacks the calculation as תו = 406, and ובהו = 11 letters/units of יט (=19) plus 11, totaling 430, matching Exodus 12:40. The year 'one thousand and seven hundred and seventy-one' (1771) dates this comment to c. 1511 CE after the destruction, or alternatively 1771 years from a earlier reckoning; preserved as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T18:53:34.792456421Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}